{"name":"Blue Calico","hex":"#a5cde1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"calico","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of rough cloth made from unbleached and not fully processed cotton, often printed with a bright pattern.","Ellipsis of calico cat."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of calico or resembling the color of calico cloth, having a pattern of red and contrasting areas; variegated.","Having red, brunette, and blond hair colors, resembling the fur of a calico cat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/calico"}],"entries":[{"title":"Calico","blocks":[{"text":"Calico is a heavy plain-woven textile made from unbleached, and often not fully processed, cotton. It may also contain unseparated husk parts. The fabric is coarser than muslin, but less coarse and thick than canvas or denim. It is still very cheap owing to its unfinished and undyed appearance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Calico_Sample.JPG/330px-Calico_Sample.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calico","wikidata":"Q1132695"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Calico_sample_Crum_%26_Co..jpg/330px-Calico_sample_Crum_%26_Co..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Calico_sample_Crum_%26_Co..jpg/330px-Calico_sample_Crum_%26_Co..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sample of calico printed with a six-colour machine by Walter Crum & Co., from Frederick Crace Calvert, Dyeing and Calico Printing (1878)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalico_sample_Crum_%26_Co..jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg/500px-Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg/1280px-Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg/500px-Calico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Calico printing (1835)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalico_printing_Wellcome_L0011294.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia